.TH TRUST 1 "September 2026" "Peios" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trust \- inspect and administer the Peios machine trust store
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trust list
.RI [ --purpose " purpose" ]
.br
.B trust list --distrusted
.br
.B trust show
.I fingerprint
.RI [ --pem ]
.br
.B trust status
.br
.B trust add
.I name file
.RI [ --purposes " purpose,..." ]
.br
.B trust remove
.I name
.br
.B trust distrust
.I fingerprint-or-file
.RI [ --reason " text" ]
.br
.B trust restore
.I fingerprint
.br
.B trust reload
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trust
queries the effective certificate set from
.BR trustd (8).
Administrative changes are written to
.BR Machine\eSystem\eTrust
in the registry, so that key's security descriptor is the authority for who
may change machine trust.
.PP
Fingerprints are SHA-256. Commands that act on a certificate already known to
the store accept an unambiguous prefix of at least eight hexadecimal digits.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List effective roots. With
.BR --purpose ,
show only roots usable for that purpose. With
.BR --distrusted ,
list fingerprints refused by local policy and their recorded reasons.
.TP
.B show
Show one effective root. The
.B --pem
option prints only its PEM certificate.
.TP
.B status
Show the daemon generation, health, source counts, compatibility mode, and
rendered paths.
.TP
.B add
Add one CA certificate from a PEM or DER file, or from standard input when
.I file
is
.BR - .
The optional comma-separated purpose list defaults to ServerAuth in the daemon.
.TP
.B remove
Remove a locally added certificate by its registry entry name.
.TP
.B distrust
Refuse a certificate regardless of whether it is shipped or locally added.
The target may be a fingerprint, an unambiguous fingerprint prefix, or a
certificate file.
.TP
.B restore
Remove a distrust decision.
.TP
.B reload
Ask the daemon to recompose the effective store immediately.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/trustd/trust.sock
Native query and control socket.
.TP
.I /usr/share/ca-certificates/mozilla.crt
Shipped CA bundle composed with local registry policy.
.TP
.I /system/retc/ssl
Rendered compatibility store.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success, 2 when a requested entry is absent, 64 for command-line usage
errors, and another nonzero status on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trustd (8),
.BR reg (1)
